---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageCards from "../../components/PageCards.astro";
import { nav } from "../../lib/nav";
import { url } from "../../lib/url";

const section = nav.find((s) => s.href === "/start/")!;
---

<Base
    title="Get started"
    description="Install ci-doctor, run it against a real failure, and check what it needs."
>
    <h1>Get started</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        The shortest path to a verdict is a log file and one command — no CI, no
        token, no model. Start there, then wire it into a pipeline.
    </p>

    <PageCards pages={section.pages} />

    <div class="note">
        <strong>On GitHub Actions there is nothing to install.</strong> One step,
        and it defaults to the run that triggered it — skip straight to
        <a href={url("/cicd/github/")}>GitHub Actions&nbsp;</a>.
    </div>

    <h2>What "getting started" actually involves</h2>
    <ol class="tight">
        <li>
            <a href={url("/start/install/")}>Install it&nbsp;</a> — or don't, on GitHub
            Actions.
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href={url("/start/first-run/")}>Replay a saved log&nbsp;</a> offline
            to see the report shape.
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href={url("/start/requirements/")}
                >Get a read-scoped token&nbsp;</a
            > when you want it to fetch a live run itself.
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href={url("/cicd/")}>Add the job to your pipeline&nbsp;</a>,
            where it runs only on failure.
        </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
        Steps 1–2 need nothing but Python. Everything after that is about
        letting it reach your CI provider. An LLM is optional at every step —
        without one you get the full deterministic report, not a stub.
    </p>
</Base>
